# Data visualisation

Data visualisation turns readings into shapes, lines and colours so that people can understand them quickly. In Home Assistant that might be the power the house is using now, how warm each room was overnight, or where everyone’s phone last reported.

A clear chart helps a household decide what to do: turn the heating down, move a sensor, or check why the freezer used more energy this week. Design every chart, dashboard and map around that decision.

This page sets the principles. These pages own the detail:

- [Chart colours](chart-colour.html) for the only chart palette
- [Charts and tables](../components/charts.html) for the operational chart specification and the **View data** table
- [Map colours](colour-maps.html) and [Maps, zones and location records](../home-assistant/maps-and-zones.html) for maps
- [Charts and data](../content/formatting-content/charts-and-data.html) for how to write quantities, units, times and gaps

## Principles

Follow these 5 principles for every chart, dashboard and map.

1. **Clarity.** Show only what the household needs for the task. Use a clear order, consistent labels and the civica styles.
2. **Accessibility.** Give everyone the same information. Do not use colour alone, meet the contrast requirements and provide the data as a table or text.
3. **Accuracy.** Show readings as Home Assistant recorded them, at even intervals, with gaps shown as gaps. Say where the data comes from.
4. **Consistency.** Give each device the same colour, name and unit everywhere, so that people learn what they are looking at.
5. **Engagement.** Make the important reading easy to find and remember, so people can act on it when it matters.

<h2 id="bg-dat-01">Tell a clear story</h2>

Before you choose a chart, write down what the person needs to know. For example: what is using power now, which room is coldest, which sensor is unavailable, or where a phone is.

1. **Know the purpose.** Decide who will look at it and what they already know. A household member checking the heating needs less detail than someone tuning an automation.
2. **Know the story.** Decide the one thing the chart should show, such as “the hall gets cold after midnight”, and arrange the chart around it.
3. **Know what matters.** Remove anything that does not help. Put the main reading where people look first.

Show the main reading without making people click to find it. A live card must stay true as the data changes, so give it a neutral title such as “Power sources”, not a fixed claim such as “Usage is falling”. Do not write a reassuring message when readings are missing.

<h2 id="bg-dat-02">Choose a visualisation type</h2>

Choose the chart from the relationship in the data, the message and who will read it. This table suggests a chart for each relationship, with a Home Assistant example.

| Relationship | Home Assistant example | Chart to use | In Home Assistant |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Time | Living room temperature over the last 24 hours | Line chart | History graph card or Statistics graph card |
| Magnitude | Energy used by each circuit today | Column or bar chart | Statistics graph card, as a bar chart |
| Rank | Devices ordered by energy used this week | Horizontal bar chart | Devices energy graph on the Energy dashboard |
| Deviation | Each room’s temperature against its target | Bar chart or dot plot | Thermostat card for one room. A table for several rooms. |
| Part-to-whole | Today’s electricity from the grid, solar panels and battery | Stacked bar or a table of shares | Energy usage graph or a table |
| Flow | Energy moving from solar panels to the battery and the home | Flow diagram | Energy distribution card, or the Sankey card where available |
| Distribution | How many nights the bedroom was below 16°C, 16 to 18°C or above 18°C this month | Column chart of ranges | No native card. Use a table. |
| Correlation | Outdoor temperature against how long the boiler ran each day | Scatter plot | No native card. Use a table. |
| Spatial | Where each household member’s phone last reported | Map | Map card |

Keep power (W or kW) and energy (Wh or kWh) on separate charts. Use a map only when location answers the question: if a list of rooms answers it, use the list. A floor plan is a diagram, not a map, so label it as a floor plan. [Energy flow and other chart families](../home-assistant/energy-flow-and-other-charts.html) owns the distribution diagram, gauges and Sankey cards.

## Charts

[Chart colours](chart-colour.html) owns the chart palette and how to use colour in a chart. Use no other chart colours.

<h3 id="bg-dat-03">Elements of a chart</h3>

Most charts share the same parts: a title, a plot area, axes with tick marks and labels, gridlines, the marks that show the data, a legend and a source. Each one helps people read the chart correctly.

This diagram labels each part on a fixed report of one hall sensor. The numbers on the diagram match the list under it.

<%=
  hex = ->(id) { govuk_colour(id).fetch('hex') }
  blue, ink, grid, paper = hex.('blue'), hex.('black'), hex.('black-tint-80'), hex.('white')
