%>

1. **Title**: in a fixed report, it states the finding.
2. **Subtitle**: the measure and unit, the sensor and the period.
3. **Legend**: names each line and shows its line style.
4. **Axis title**: gives the unit once, so the labels do not repeat it.
5. **Axis labels**: the values on the scale.
6. **Tick marks**: show where each night sits on the axis.
7. **Gridlines**: help people read a value across the chart. Keep them quiet.
8. **Plot area**: the space inside the axes where the data is drawn.
9. **Marks**: the line and points that show each reading.
10. **Annotation**: explains one point, next to that point.
11. **Source**: the entity and the integration the readings came from.

#### Titles

Give every chart a title, and a subtitle when the chart is published as a fixed report.

On a live dashboard card, name what is measured, such as “Hall temperature” or “Power now”, because the data will change under the title. In a fixed report, the title can state the main finding.

Write titles that are:

- front-loaded, with the most important words first
- in the active voice
- in sentence case
- as short as possible

Give a fixed report a subtitle with the measure and unit, the rooms or devices covered and the period. For example:

> **The hall fell below 18°C on 3 nights this week**
>
> Hourly mean temperature in °C, hall sensor, 1 to 7 November 2026

#### Axes

Axes show what is measured, such as time, temperature or energy. Keep Home Assistant’s native axes and units.

- Put the unit in the axis title or subtitle, such as “Temperature (°C)”, not after every label. Put `%` and `£` in the labels themselves.
- Do not repeat the chart title in the axis title.
- Keep device and room names short. Use the friendly name the household set, not the entity ID.

#### Annotations

Use an annotation to explain one point, such as “Boiler service, no readings”.

- Keep it to one sentence of about 10 words.
- Put it next to the point it explains, with space around it so it does not cover the data.
- Repeat anything essential in the text or the table, because annotations are hard to reach with a screen reader.
- Do not add a fixed annotation that claims something the integration did not supply.

#### Sources and footnotes

Say where every chart’s data comes from. Use this format: [entity or statistic] from [integration]. For example: “Source: Hall temperature from the Zigbee Home Automation integration, Home Assistant long-term statistics.”

Use a footnote only for context that changes how people read the chart, such as “No readings from 02:00 to 03:10 while the hub restarted.” Too many footnotes interrupt the reading.

<h3 id="bg-dat-04">Keep the readings true</h3>

Show readings exactly as Home Assistant supplies them: native values, IDs, timestamps, units and gaps.

- Show a missing reading as a gap, never as 0.
- Do not smooth away a peak or force a line chart to start at zero.
- Keep a comparison on its original dates, such as this week against the same days last week.
- Say in words when a value is a forecast, a comparison or an export shown as a negative number.

[Charts and tables](../components/charts.html#visual-rules) explains how the civica Energy adapter keeps native values while it restyles the chart.

<h3 id="bg-dat-05">Use the chart specification</h3>

Operational Energy charts follow [Charts and tables](../components/charts.html) and take their colours from [Chart colours](chart-colour.html). Those pages own line widths, legend keys, hatching and how colours stay the same with every theme colour. Hatching, markers and line styles are cues that do not depend on colour, so the [illustration](illustration.html) limits on texture and outline do not apply to them.

<h3 id="bg-dat-06">Interactive charts</h3>

An interactive chart lets people change what it shows, for example by choosing a date, hiding a source or zooming in.

Use interaction when:

- people want to see their own detail, such as one room or one device
- there is no clear way to show the data without it
- different household members care about different rooms or devices

Interaction has costs. People must act before they see the information, so they can miss the main message. If interaction is not needed, show the main finding in a fixed chart, or use several small charts side by side instead of one crowded chart.

Keep the native interaction Home Assistant already gives: the date picker, legend toggles, zoom, tooltips and keyboard controls. Do not replace a working native chart with an image people cannot use. civica does not add a second chart renderer. [Compatibility and limits](../get-started/compatibility.html) explains which frontend versions the Energy adapter supports.

<h3 id="bg-dat-07">Give a text alternative</h3>

Give every chart a table or a sentence with the same facts. For supported Energy charts, the **View data** table does this. [Charts and tables](../components/charts.html#accessible-values) owns what that table contains.

For a new chart or a fixed report, add a matching table or a short summary, such as “Hall temperature, 10 to 11 November 2026: lowest 17.8°C at 03:20, highest 21.5°C at 18:05.” Label invented example data as invented. Test the chart and its alternative separately, because a good table does not prove the chart’s focus order or colours work.

### Tips for publishing charts

Some places accept only certain image sizes or file formats. Check what a forum, a release page or a social media platform accepts before you export a chart.

## Dashboards

A dashboard brings several cards together to give an overview, such as the energy the home is using, the temperature in each room and which doors are open. It updates as readings arrive, so people come back to it many times a day.

### When to use a dashboard

Use a dashboard when:

- people need a quick overview, not a detailed explanation
- the information matters every day and people already know what it means
- the data changes often and people check it again and again
- Home Assistant updates the readings automatically

Base the dashboard on what the household actually checks. [Home and rooms](../patterns/home.html) owns household overview dashboards.

### Disadvantages of dashboards

A dashboard is not always the right answer. Dashboards often:

- leave people to work out what the readings mean
- cannot explain data that needs a lot of context
- go out of date as the household’s needs change
- need regular care when devices are added or renamed
- are hard to make work well on a phone, a tablet on the wall and a desktop

To explain something in depth, such as why the energy bill went up, use a page with a few related charts and text instead.

### Tips

- Put the most important cards first, such as current power or open doors, and group the rest by room or task.
- Give each card a short explanation where people need it, without crowding the reading.
- Prefer native cards. A custom card may not support keyboard access, the civica theme or a data table, so check before you rely on it.
- Check the dashboard on a phone, a tablet and a desktop, with long device names and many devices. [Layout and spacing](layout.html) owns the dashboard grid.

<h3 id="bg-dat-09">Keep reports apart from live dashboards</h3>

A fixed report, such as a monthly energy summary for a household newsletter, can show one result with a large number or a simple shape. Give its date, what it counts, how it is rounded and its source, as real text. Do not use size, area or motion to exaggerate the result.

A live dashboard needs accurate current states and comparisons that repeat reliably. Do not bring celebratory colours, gradients, oversized lines or claims from a report into it.

<h2 id="bg-dat-08">Maps</h2>

Use a map when location answers the household’s question, such as which zone a phone is in. [Map colours](colour-maps.html) owns the map palette and shows a working OpenStreetMap example. [Maps, zones and location records](../home-assistant/maps-and-zones.html) owns how a map behaves in Home Assistant.

### Elements of a map

Every map needs these parts. The owning page explains each one.

| Element | What it does | Detail |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Title | Says what the map shows, neutrally if the data changes, such as “Reported locations”. | [Title, source and observation context](../home-assistant/maps-and-zones.html#map-context) |
| Labels | Names each marker or zone on the map, so a few features need no legend. | [Map hierarchy, extent and scale](../home-assistant/maps-and-zones.html#map-composition) |
| Legend | Explains several categories, placed before the map at its upper left. | [Map hierarchy, extent and scale](../home-assistant/maps-and-zones.html#map-composition) |
| Base map | Gives context, such as streets and place names, without competing with the markers. | [Using colour combinations on maps](colour-maps.html#using-colour-combinations-on-maps) |
| Source | Names the tracker that reported each location and the provider of the base map. | [Title, source and observation context](../home-assistant/maps-and-zones.html#map-context) |

### Map considerations

- **Hierarchy:** make the household’s markers the strongest thing on the map. Keep the base map quiet.
- **Fewer features:** leave out anything that does not help the question. Keep the provider’s place names and attribution.
- **Extent:** fit the first view to the places the task needs, such as home and the school zone, not the whole country.
- **Scale:** add a scale bar only when distance matters, such as how far a phone is from home.

### Map projections

Use one projection for each map. Do not mix projections, or lay a map in one projection over a base map in another.

- **Street maps:** use the projection of the base map provider. OpenStreetMap tiles use Web Mercator, so zone circles, markers and tracker positions line up with the streets.
- **Household and floor plan maps:** use a flat plan with no projection, drawn to a single scale. A floor plan shows rooms, not places on the earth, so do not add latitude and longitude to it.
- Do not put a floor plan on a street map, or street markers on a floor plan, unless you have placed and scaled the plan to match.

### Showing scale

Show scale when distance matters, such as how far a phone is from the Home zone. Use a scale bar with a plain label, such as “500m”, not a ratio. On a floor plan, say what the bar measures, such as “1m”. If the map zooms, the bar must change with it. Leave the scale out when people only need to know which zone a phone is in.

### Remove extra detail

Show only what answers the question. On a street map, hide the base map layers the household does not need, such as shop names or transport routes. On a floor plan, show the rooms and the devices on the dashboard, not every wall socket. Keep the provider’s attribution. Detail that people cannot read at the size of the card is noise, so remove it.

### Map accessibility

- Say in text what the map shows. For a complex map, add a longer description nearby.
- Give the same records as a list or table, with each place, its source and when it was reported. [Every record remains reachable](../home-assistant/maps-and-zones.html#map-records) owns this.
- Use letters, shapes or line styles as well as colour, so that people who cannot tell colours apart can still read the map.
- Check the contrast of markers, lines and labels against the real base map.
- When you can, test the map with people who use a screen reader or other assistive technology.

Do not recolour map tiles, cover a weather layer, or move a marker to fit a layout. The Energy adapter does not restyle native map cards.

