  svg << marker_a.(18, 96, 8) << %(<text x="30" y="100" font-size="10" fill="#{ink}">Rhys's phone</text>) << marker_b.(18, 114, 8) << %(<text x="30" y="118" font-size="10" fill="#{ink}">Ffion's phone</text>)
  svg << %(<line x1="10" y1="226" x2="70" y2="226" stroke="#{ink}" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="10" y1="221" x2="10" y2="231" stroke="#{ink}" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="70" y1="221" x2="70" y2="231" stroke="#{ink}" stroke-width="2"/><text x="76" y="230" font-size="10" fill="#{ink}">500m</text>)
  svg << %(<text x="0" y="252" font-size="10" fill="#{ink}">Source: Companion app. Base map drawn for this example.</text>)
  records = [
    ["Rhys's phone", 'A, circle', 'In town, 1.1km from Home', '07:36', '20 metres', 'device_tracker.rhys_phone'],
    ["Ffion's phone", 'B, diamond', 'Home zone', '07:38', '12 metres', 'device_tracker.ffion_phone'],
    ["Owen's phone", 'Not on the map', 'Not supplied. This does not mean Owen is away.', 'No report today', 'Not supplied', 'device_tracker.owen_phone']
  ]
  body = records.map { |r| %(<tr class="govuk-table__row"><th scope="row" class="govuk-table__header">#{r[0]}<br><code>#{r[5]}</code></th>#{r[1..4].map { |c| %(<td class="govuk-table__cell">#{c}</td>) }.join}</tr>) }.join
  %(<div class="c-colour-example"><figure style="margin:0 0 20px">) +
    %(<svg role="img" aria-labelledby="map-example-title map-example-desc" viewBox="0 0 320 260" style="display:block;width:100%;max-width:480px;height:auto;font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif"><title id="map-example-title">Map of the reported locations of 2 phones</title><desc id="map-example-desc">An invented coastline with land at the top left and sea at the bottom right. The Home zone is a circle near the coast. Diamond B, Ffion's phone, is inside the Home zone. Circle A, Rhys's phone, is in town, about 1.1km to the south-west. A legend at the upper left names the zone and the 2 markers, and a scale bar shows 500m. Owen's phone has no reported location and is not drawn. The table after the map lists all 3 phones.</desc><g aria-hidden="true">#{svg.join}</g></svg>) +
    %(<figcaption>A drawing in the map colours, not a real place. The household, phones and locations are invented, and no device is tracked.</figcaption></figure>) +
    %(<div class="civica-table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="All 3 phones and their location records"><table class="govuk-table"><caption class="govuk-table__caption govuk-table__caption--s">All 3 phones and their location records, 17 November 2026 (invented)</caption><thead class="govuk-table__head"><tr class="govuk-table__row"><th scope="col" class="govuk-table__header">Phone</th><th scope="col" class="govuk-table__header">Marker</th><th scope="col" class="govuk-table__header">Reported location</th><th scope="col" class="govuk-table__header">Reported at</th><th scope="col" class="govuk-table__header">Accuracy</th></tr></thead><tbody class="govuk-table__body">#{body}</tbody></table></div></div>)
%>

[Map colours](colour-maps.html) lists every colour in this drawing, with values to copy.

## Examples

These examples use invented households and simulated data. They do not show a real home or operate devices.

### Energy: power now

This working chart uses the civica Energy adapter with invented readings. Notice the separate unfilled line for each source, the legend that matches each line, and the **View data** table below the chart.

<%= example('bg-data-operational-power') %>

### Climate: room temperatures over a day

A line chart can compare the temperature in each room across one day, with the target temperature for reference. Notice that each room has its own line style and a label at the end of its line, that the target is a thin black step line, and that the missing bedroom reading at 12:00 is a gap, not a drop to 0.

<%=
  hex = ->(id) { govuk_colour(id).fetch('hex') }
  ink, grid = hex.('black'), hex.('black-tint-80')
  times = %w[00:00 03:00 06:00 09:00 12:00 15:00 18:00 21:00]
  rooms = [
    ['Living room', 'sensor.living_room_temperature', 'blue', nil, 'solid line', [17.2, 16.6, 16.4, 19.6, 20.1, 20.4, 20.8, 20.3], 108],
    ['Kitchen', 'sensor.kitchen_temperature', 'magenta', '8 4', 'dashed line', [16.9, 16.1, 16.3, 20.2, 19.4, 19.0, 21.3, 19.9], 124],
    ['Bedroom', 'sensor.bedroom_temperature', 'teal-tint-25', '2 3', 'dotted line', [18.1, 17.6, 17.3, 18.4, nil, 17.9, 18.6, 18.9], 146]
  ]
  x_of = ->(h) { (34 + h * 200.0 / 21).round(1) }
  y_of = ->(v) { (250 - (v - 14) * 22).round(1) }
  svg = []
  rooms.each_with_index do |(name, _, colour, dash, _, _, _), i|
    lx = [0, 110, 0][i]; ly = [12, 12, 30][i]
    svg << %(<line x1="#{lx}" y1="#{ly}" x2="#{lx + 24}" y2="#{ly}" stroke="#{hex.(colour)}" stroke-width="3"#{%( stroke-dasharray="#{dash}") if dash}/><text x="#{lx + 30}" y="#{ly + 4}" font-size="11" fill="#{ink}">#{name}</text>)
  end
  svg << %(<line x1="110" y1="30" x2="134" y2="30" stroke="#{ink}" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="12 4"/><text x="140" y="34" font-size="11" fill="#{ink}">Heating target</text>)
  svg << %(<text x="0" y="62" font-size="12" fill="#{ink}">°C</text>)
  [14, 16, 18, 20, 22].each { |v| svg << %(<line x1="34" y1="#{y_of.(v)}" x2="234" y2="#{y_of.(v)}" stroke="#{grid}" stroke-width="1"/><text x="28" y="#{y_of.(v) + 4}" font-size="11" text-anchor="end" fill="#{ink}">#{v}</text>) }
  [0, 6, 12, 18].each { |h| svg << %(<text x="#{x_of.(h)}" y="268" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#{ink}">#{format('%02d:00', h)}</text>) }
  svg << %(<path d="M34 #{y_of.(16)} H#{x_of.(6)} V#{y_of.(20)} H234" fill="none" stroke="#{ink}" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="12 4"/><text x="38" y="#{y_of.(20) - 6}" font-size="10" fill="#{ink}">Target</text>)
  rooms.each do |name, id, colour, dash, _, values, label_y|
    segments = values.each_with_index.chunk_while { |(a, _), (b, _)| a && b }.map { |run| run.reject { |v, _| v.nil? } }.reject(&:empty?)
    segments.each do |run|
      d = run.map { |v, i| "#{x_of.(i * 3)} #{y_of.(v)}" }.join(' L')
      svg << %(<path data-series="#{id}" d="M#{d}" fill="none" stroke="#{hex.(colour)}" stroke-width="3"#{%( stroke-dasharray="#{dash}") if dash}/>)
    end
    svg << %(<text x="240" y="#{label_y}" font-size="11" fill="#{ink}">#{name}</text>)
  end
  svg << %(<text x="#{x_of.(12) + 2}" y="166" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" fill="#{ink}">No reading</text>)
  head = %(<tr class="govuk-table__row"><th scope="col" class="govuk-table__header">Time</th>) + rooms.map { |r| %(<th scope="col" class="govuk-table__header">#{r[0]}</th>) }.join + %(<th scope="col" class="govuk-table__header">Heating target</th></tr>)
  body = times.each_with_index.map do |t, i|
    cells = rooms.map { |r| v = r[5][i]; %(<td class="govuk-table__cell">#{v ? format('%.1f', v) : 'No reading'}</td>) }.join
    %(<tr class="govuk-table__row"><th scope="row" class="govuk-table__header">#{t}</th>#{cells}<td class="govuk-table__cell">#{i < 2 ? '16.0' : '20.0'}</td></tr>)
  end
  %(<div class="c-colour-example"><figure style="margin:0 0 20px">) +
    %(<svg role="img" aria-labelledby="climate-title climate-desc" viewBox="0 0 320 280" style="display:block;width:100%;max-width:480px;height:auto;font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif"><title id="climate-title">Line chart of 3 room temperatures and the heating target, in degrees Celsius, over one day</title><desc id="climate-desc">The living room is a solid line, the kitchen dashed and the bedroom dotted. The heating target is 16°C until 06:00 and 20°C after it. The living room and kitchen reach the target in the afternoon and evening. The bedroom stays between 17.3°C and 18.9°C all day. There is no bedroom reading at 12:00, shown as a gap. The table after the chart gives every reading.</desc><g aria-hidden="true">#{svg.join}</g></svg>) +
    %(<figcaption>Room temperatures (°C) every 3 hours, Tuesday 17 November 2026, Europe/London. Invented readings for the Hughes household. The heating target comes from <code>climate.hughes_heating</code>, and is 20°C from 06:00 to 22:00.</figcaption></figure>) +
    %(<details class="govuk-details"><summary class="govuk-details__summary"><span class="govuk-details__summary-text">Show the data: room temperatures, 17 November 2026</span></summary><div class="govuk-details__text"><div class="civica-table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Room temperatures and heating target"><table class="govuk-table"><caption class="govuk-table__caption govuk-table__caption--s">Room temperatures and heating target (°C), 17 November 2026 (invented readings)</caption><thead class="govuk-table__head">#{head}</thead><tbody class="govuk-table__body">#{body.join}</tbody></table></div><p>Entities: #{rooms.map { |r| "<code>#{r[1]}</code> (#{r[4]})" }.join(', ')}.</p></div></details></div>)
%>

In Home Assistant, a History graph card that shows a thermostat's climate entity draws its target temperature next to the room temperature. The card draws solid lines only, so give each entity a clear name for the legend.

### A dashboard on a tablet on the wall

A dashboard for a tablet on the wall shows the few readings a household checks most, large enough to read from across the room. Notice that each card has one large reading with its unit and one line of context, and that the hour still in progress on the energy chart is drawn as an outline, not as a full bar.

<%=
  hex = ->(id) { govuk_colour(id).fetch('hex') }
  ink, grid, quiet, paper, blue = hex.('black'), hex.('black-tint-80'), hex.('black-tint-95'), hex.('white'), hex.('blue')
  hours = [0.31, 0.28, 0.27, 0.29, 0.30, 0.35, 0.62, 0.88]
  tiles = [['Power now', '1.42kW', 'From the grid'], ['Hall', '18.6°C', 'Target 20°C'], ['Back door', 'Open', 'Since 07:31']]
  svg = []
  svg << %(<rect x="0" y="0" width="360" height="240" rx="14" fill="#{ink}"/><rect x="12" y="12" width="336" height="216" fill="#{paper}"/><rect x="12" y="12" width="336" height="28" fill="#{quiet}"/>)
  svg << %(<text x="22" y="31" font-size="13" font-weight="700" fill="#{ink}">Hughes home</text><text x="338" y="31" font-size="11" text-anchor="end" fill="#{ink}">Tuesday 17 November, 07:40</text>)
  tiles.each_with_index do |(label, value, note), i|
    x = 20 + i * 109
    svg << %(<rect x="#{x}" y="48" width="101" height="78" fill="#{paper}" stroke="#{grid}" stroke-width="1"/><text x="#{x + 8}" y="66" font-size="12" fill="#{ink}">#{label}</text><text x="#{x + 8}" y="98" font-size="24" font-weight="700" fill="#{ink}">#{value}</text><text x="#{x + 8}" y="117" font-size="11" fill="#{ink}">#{note}</text>)
  end
  svg << %(<rect x="20" y="134" width="320" height="86" fill="#{paper}" stroke="#{grid}" stroke-width="1"/><text x="28" y="151" font-size="12" fill="#{ink}">Energy used today (kWh)</text><text x="332" y="151" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end" fill="#{ink}">3.30kWh so far</text>)
  svg << %(<line x1="28" y1="200" x2="332" y2="200" stroke="#{grid}" stroke-width="1"/>)
  hours.each_with_index do |v, i|
    x = 36 + i * 37; h = (v * 40).round(1)
    style = i == 7 ? %(fill="#{paper}" stroke="#{blue}" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 2") : %(fill="#{blue}")
    svg << %(<rect x="#{x}" y="#{(200 - h).round(1)}" width="24" height="#{h}" #{style}/>)
    svg << %(<text x="#{x + 12}" y="213" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" fill="#{ink}">#{format('%02d', i)}#{' so far' if i == 7}</text>) if i.even? || i == 7
  end
  rows = hours.each_with_index.map { |v, i| %(<tr class="govuk-table__row"><th scope="row" class="govuk-table__header">#{format('%02d:00', i)} to #{i == 7 ? '07:40, still in progress' : format('%02d:00', i + 1)}</th><td class="govuk-table__cell">#{format('%.2f', v)}</td></tr>) }
  %(<div class="c-colour-example"><figure style="margin:0 0 20px">) +
    %(<svg role="img" aria-labelledby="tablet-title tablet-desc" viewBox="0 0 360 240" style="display:block;width:100%;max-width:560px;height:auto;font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif"><title id="tablet-title">Drawing of a dashboard on a wall tablet</title><desc id="tablet-desc">A landscape tablet shows the Hughes home dashboard at 07:40 on Tuesday 17 November. 3 cards show power now, 1.42kW from the grid; the hall, 18.6°C with a target of 20°C; and the back door, open since 07:31. A wide card shows energy used today in hourly bars, 3.30kWh so far, with the hour from 07:00 drawn as an outline because it is still in progress. The table after the drawing gives every value.</desc><g aria-hidden="true">#{svg.join}</g></svg>) +
    %(<figcaption>A drawing of a wall tablet dashboard, not a screenshot. The household and readings are invented. Entities: <code>sensor.grid_power</code>, <code>sensor.hall_temperature</code>, <code>binary_sensor.back_door</code> and <code>sensor.energy_today</code>.</figcaption></figure>) +
    %(<details class="govuk-details"><summary class="govuk-details__summary"><span class="govuk-details__summary-text">Show the data: wall tablet dashboard at 07:40</span></summary><div class="govuk-details__text"><div class="civica-table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Wall tablet dashboard readings"><table class="govuk-table"><caption class="govuk-table__caption govuk-table__caption--s">Readings at 07:40, 17 November 2026 (invented)</caption><thead class="govuk-table__head"><tr class="govuk-table__row"><th scope="col" class="govuk-table__header">Card</th><th scope="col" class="govuk-table__header">Reading</th></tr></thead><tbody class="govuk-table__body">#{tiles.map { |l, v, n| %(<tr class="govuk-table__row"><th scope="row" class="govuk-table__header">#{l}</th><td class="govuk-table__cell">#{v}, #{n.sub(/\A\w/, &:downcase)}</td></tr>) }.join}</tbody></table></div><div class="civica-table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Energy used today by hour"><table class="govuk-table"><caption class="govuk-table__caption govuk-table__caption--s">Energy used today by hour (kWh)</caption><thead class="govuk-table__head"><tr class="govuk-table__row"><th scope="col" class="govuk-table__header">Hour</th><th scope="col" class="govuk-table__header">Energy (kWh)</th></tr></thead><tbody class="govuk-table__body">#{rows.join}</tbody><tfoot><tr class="govuk-table__row"><th scope="row" class="govuk-table__header">Total so far</th><td class="govuk-table__cell">3.30</td></tr></tfoot></table></div></div></details></div>)
%>

Power now is in kW and energy today is in kWh, so they are on separate cards. [Layout and spacing](layout.html) owns how the cards fit a tablet screen.

